// Regression test: Codegen's independent type-inference (getExprType(),
// used for casts/argument-type resolution -- separate from and never
// reading TypeChecker's own results) must correctly substitute a generic
// FREE function's return type at each call site, not report the bare,
// unsubstituted type parameter.
//
// Before this fix, `fn<T> firstElem(arr: T[]): T` compiled fine on its
// own, but `firstElem(intArray) to string` failed with "I can't convert
// 'T' to a string!" even though TypeChecker's own (separate) inference
// already correctly resolved the call's type as int -- Codegen's
// getExprType() registered the function under its bare declared return
// type (TurfGenericParamType("T"), since a generic function is type-
// erased to one compiled version) and returned it completely
// unsubstituted. Fixed by unifying each declared parameter type against
// the actual argument type at the call site (recursing through array/
// pointer wrapping) to recover T, then substituting it into the return
// type.

import io;

import test;
fn<T> firstElem(arr: T[]): T {
    return arr[0];
}

fn main(): int {
    mut intArray: int[3] = [10, 20, 30];
    test::assert(firstElem(intArray) == 10, "firstElem should return the first element");
    io::printline(firstElem(intArray) to string);
    return 0;
}
